<Lesson
  id="tm"
  dek="The protocol that took 1990s Byzantine fault tolerance and made it run a public, proof-of-stake blockchain, where every committed block is final."
  before={['Byzantine Generals', 'PBFT', 'Bitcoin']}
  papers={papers}
>
  <TendermintSim slot="sim" />

  <h2>The problem it solved</h2>
  <p>
    By 2014 there were two working answers to "how do strangers agree?", and neither fit a proof-of-stake blockchain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>PBFT</strong> (1999) gave fast, final agreement, but for a fixed cluster of servers run by one organization
    and wired directly to each other. <strong>Bitcoin</strong> (2008) let anyone join, but its blocks are never
    strictly final. A deeper chain can always replace them, so you wait for "confirmations" and accept a small risk.
  </p>
  <p>
    Jae Kwon's question was: if validators are identified by the coins they lock up as stake, can they run PBFT-style
    voting over the open internet and get blocks that are final the moment they are committed? Tendermint is the
    answer.
  </p>

  <h2>How one height works</h2>
  <p>Each block height is decided in one or more <strong>rounds</strong>. A round has three steps:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Propose.</strong> A designated proposer broadcasts a block. The proposer rotates, weighted by stake, so no
      validator leads for long.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prevote.</strong> Each validator gossips a prevote for the block, or for <code>nil</code> if it saw no
      valid block in time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Precommit.</strong> A validator that sees prevotes from more than 2/3 of voting power for the same block
      (a <em>polka</em>) locks on that block and precommits it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Once more than 2/3 precommit the same block, it is <strong>committed</strong> and final. If a round fails because the
    proposer was offline or votes split, everyone times out and starts the next round with the next proposer.
  </p>

  <h2>Why "more than 2/3"?</h2>
  <p>
    Assume fewer than 1/3 of voting power is Byzantine. Any two groups that each hold more than 2/3 of the power must
    overlap in more than 1/3. That overlap can't be entirely Byzantine, so it contains at least one honest validator,
    and an honest validator never votes for two different blocks at the same step. So two conflicting blocks can never
    both collect a quorum.
  </p>
  <p>
    This <em>quorum intersection</em> argument is the same one that makes Paxos work with simple majorities. Tendermint
    needs the bigger 2/3 quorums because some voters may lie.
  </p>

  <h2>The clever part: locking</h2>
  <p>
    Rounds create a danger. Suppose a few validators precommit block A in round 0, but not enough for a commit, and in
    round 1 the network moves to block B. If A had in fact been committed somewhere, choosing B would fork the chain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tendermint prevents this with <strong>locks</strong>. A validator that precommits a block locks on it, and in later
    rounds it prevotes only that block. It releases the lock only if it sees a polka for a different block in a
    later round, which proves the network has safely moved on. Locks are what make it safe to change proposers again
    and again without ever committing two blocks at the same height.
  </p>

  <h2>Accountability</h2>
  <p>
    Every vote is signed. A validator who signs two conflicting votes at the same height and round leaves
    cryptographic evidence that anyone can submit to the chain, and its stake can be <em>slashed</em>. Bitcoin can't do
    this: a miner who builds on two forks hasn't signed anything. Tendermint makes misbehavior provable and costly.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Safety over liveness.</strong> If more than 1/3 of voting power goes offline, the chain stops. It never
      forks. Try "V3 + V4 offline" in the animation above. Bitcoin makes the opposite choice: it keeps producing blocks
      during a network split and resolves forks later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quadratic messages.</strong> Every validator's vote reaches every other validator, so traffic grows with
      the square of the validator count. That's one reason Tendermint chains typically run with up to a few hundred
      validators.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not optimistically responsive.</strong> After a failed round, a new proposer has to wait out a timeout
      sized for the worst-case network delay, even when the network is fast. HotStuff (2018) was designed largely to
      remove that wait.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it runs today</h2>
  <p>
    Tendermint Core became the engine of the Cosmos ecosystem, and dozens of independent chains run it. In 2023 the
    maintained implementation was renamed <strong>CometBFT</strong>. Celestia runs on celestia-core, a fork of
    CometBFT.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="side">
    <div class="box">
      <h3>How it compares</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Who can join</th><th>Finality</th><th>If too many fail</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PBFT</td><td>fixed set</td><td>immediate</td><td>halts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bitcoin</td><td>anyone</td><td>probabilistic</td><td>keeps going, may fork</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tendermint</td><td>stakers</td><td>immediate</td><td>halts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Quiz
      question="Why does a Tendermint vote need more than 2/3 of voting power, not just a majority?"
      options={[
        { text: 'Because a majority is too slow to collect' },
        { text: 'So any two quorums overlap in at least one honest validator, even with up to 1/3 lying', correct: true },
        { text: 'Because 2/3 of validators are always online' },
      ]}
      right="Right. Two quorums of more than 2/3 overlap in more than 1/3, which must include an honest validator, and honest validators never vote for two blocks."
      wrong="Not quite. Speed and uptime aren’t the reason. It’s quorum intersection: two quorums of more than 2/3 always share an honest validator."
    />
    <Quiz
      question="Half the validators go offline. What does Tendermint do?"
      options={[
        { text: 'Keeps producing blocks and resolves forks later' },
        { text: 'Halts until enough validators return, without forking', correct: true },
        { text: 'Lowers the quorum to 1/2 automatically' },
      ]}
      right="Right. Tendermint prefers safety: with 1/2 offline, no vote can reach the 2/3 quorum, so it waits."
      wrong="Not quite. That describes Bitcoin. Tendermint never lowers its quorum, so it halts rather than risk a fork."
    />
  </Fragment>
</Lesson>
